'''Jewish Babylonian Aramaic''' ([[Aramaic]]: {{lang|tmr|ארמית}} {{Transliteration|tmr|Ārāmît}}) was the form of [[Aramaic language#Middle Aramaic|Middle Aramaic]] employed by writers in [[Lower Mesopotamia]] between the fourth and eleventh centuries. It is most commonly identified with the language of the [[Talmud|Babylonian Talmud]] (which was completed in the fifth century), the [[Targum Onqelos]], and of post-Talmudic ([[Geonim|Gaonic]]) literature, which are the most important cultural products of [[History of the Jews in Iraq|Babylonian Jews]]. The most important [[Epigraphy|epigraphic]] sources for the dialect are the hundreds of inscriptions on [[incantation bowl]]s.{{sfn|Sokoloff|2003}}

==Classification and type==
[[File:Zauberschale aus Mesopotamien.jpg|thumb|Incantation bowl inscribed in Babylonian Aramaic, using [[Hebrew alphabet|Hebrew square-script]], dated between 400 and 800, in the collection of the [[Jewish Museum of Switzerland]].]]
The language was closely related to Eastern Aramaic varieties such as [[Mandaic language|Mandaic]]. Its original pronunciation is uncertain and has to be reconstructed with the help of these kindred dialects and the reading tradition of the [[Yemenite Jews]],{{sfn|Morag|1988}} and where available those of the [[History of the Jews in Iraq|Iraqi]], [[Syrian Jews|Syrian]] and [[History of the Jews in Egypt|Egyptian Jews]].{{citation needed|date=April 2022}} The value of the [[Yemenite Hebrew|Yemenite reading tradition]] has been challenged by [[Matthew Morgenstern]].{{sfn|Morgenstern|2011}} Vocalized Aramaic texts with which Jews are familiar, from the [[Tanakh]] and the [[siddur]]im, are of limited usefulness for this purpose, as they are in different dialects.{{sfn|Bar-Asher Siegal|2013}}

Talmudic Aramaic bears all the marks of being a specialist language of study and legal argumentation like [[Law French]]{{citation needed|date=April 2022}} rather than a vernacular mother tongue,{{citation needed|date=April 2022}} and continued in use for these purposes long after [[Judeo-Arabic languages]] had become used in daily life. It has developed a battery of technical logic terms such as {{Transliteration|tmr|tiyuvta}} "conclusive refutation" and {{Transliteration|tmr|tiqu}} "undecidable moot point", which are still used in Jewish legal writings, including those in other languages, and have influenced [[modern Hebrew]].{{citation needed|date=April 2022}}

Like the other [[Judeo-Aramaic languages]], it was written in the [[Hebrew alphabet]].

