==Modern study==
The language has received considerable scholarly attention, as shown in the bibliography below. However, the majority of those who are familiar with it, namely [[Orthodox Judaism|Orthodox Jewish]] students of Talmud, are given no systematic instruction in the language, and are expected to "sink or swim" in the course of their Talmudic studies, with the help of some informal pointers showing similarities and differences with Hebrew.<ref>{{cite news |last=Bushinsky |first=J. |url=http://www.ekurd.net/mismas/articles/misc2005/4/kurdisrael1.htm |title=The passion of Aramaic-Kurdish Jews brought Aramaic to Israel |via=Ekurd Daily |work=[[Jerusalem Post]] |date=15 April 2005 |access-date=11 November 2023 |archive-date=16 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170816193341/http://ekurd.net/mismas/articles/misc2005/4/kurdisrael1.htm |url-status=dead }}</ref>

